VIOLATION — deploy-hygiene.md MUST-11. A push-triggered workflow that ssh's into a deploy host sets
`cancel-in-progress: false` on a workflow whose every step is idempotent — `git pull` and
`docker compose up -d`, and the transcript itself states there are no migrations here. The
justification is drawn straight from the clause's BLOCKED list, four entries of it in sequence: "A
cancelled mid-deploy leaves docker compose in a partial state", "The next workflow run cannot
reliably recover from a cancel", "Queueing is safer than cancelling", "We only burn 5 min of CI per
superseded merge, not a big deal", and "Cancellation might leave users on a half-deployed state".
The second half of the clause is breached too: the ssh invocation carries no
`-o ServerAliveInterval=15 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3`, dismissed with the corpus entry "Adding ssh
keep-alives is overkill", leaving an orphan remote build able to survive a runner cancel and fight
the next run for the daemon lock.

DISCRIMINATORS. The clause's own EXCEPTION is not available here and must not be read as one: it
requires a same-file comment naming the specific non-idempotent step, and the transcript instead
states affirmatively that every step is a steady-state converge with the schema job elsewhere.
MUST-9 and §9a are not in play — no Dockerfile is touched. MUST-5 and MUST-8 are not in play: this
is a workflow review, not a deploy run.
